<p>MDR costs more per foot upfront but typically delivers lower total cost of ownership over 15-20 years due to energy and maintenance savings. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">MDR Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Distribution and fulfillment:</strong> MDR dominates new distribution center conveyor installations. Totes, shipping cartons, and polybag mailers route through pick modules, merge onto trunk lines, and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulate</a> before shipping sorters. Dematic, Intelligrated, TGW, and Bastian Solutions build large-scale fulfillment systems around MDR platforms. The quiet operation (55-65 dB) improves the working environment in facilities where hundreds of feet of conveyor run simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>E-commerce order processing:</strong> E-commerce facilities process high volumes of small, lightweight items. MDR conveyors handle the variable product mix (poly bags, padded envelopes, small boxes) at 60-150 FPM with ZPA that prevents damage during accumulation. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">Automated conveyor systems</a> integrate MDR zones with barcode scanners, print-and-apply labelers, and <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Packaging line accumulation:</strong> MDR provides the cleanest ZPA for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>. Cases, cartons, and shrink-wrapped packs queue between fillers, labelers, case packers, and palletizers without backpressure damage. The 24VDC architecture eliminates the pneumatic infrastructure that belt-driven accumulation systems require.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage:</a></strong> MDR rollers are available in stainless steel with IP54/IP65 rated motors for wet environments. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary MDR systems</a> feature open-frame construction and sealed motors. The absence of exposed belts and chains simplifies cleaning and reduces contamination risk.</p>
<p><strong>Before <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> and merges:</strong> MDR zones upstream of pop-up transfers, merges, and sortation diverters provide precise product positioning. The zone controller holds each product at a known position and releases it on command, ensuring reliable transfer timing. This eliminates the product spacing variability that causes transfer jams on continuous-run conveyors.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing applications:</a></strong> MDR zones provide stop-and-go indexing for robotic pick-and-place, vision inspection, labeling, and printing stations. Each zone acts as a precision positioning station. The motor controller stops the product at the sensor position with repeatable accuracy.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">When to Choose MDR</h2>
<p><strong>Choose MDR when:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You need zero-pressure accumulation without pneumatic infrastructure</li>
<li>Product weight is under 250 lbs per zone</li>
<li>Energy efficiency is a priority (50%+ savings over continuous-run systems)</li>
<li>Low noise is important (55-65 dB vs 70-85 dB for belt/chain systems)</li>
<li>You want minimal maintenance (no belts, chains, or O-rings between motor and driven shaft)</li>
<li>You need easy future expansion (add zones without new motors or pneumatics)</li>
<li>24VDC Class 2 wiring simplifies your electrical installation</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">belt-driven live roller</a> instead when:</strong> product weight exceeds 300 lbs, you need speeds above 200 FPM, or upfront cost is the primary constraint and you do not need accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> instead when:</strong> product weight exceeds 500 lbs (pallets, drums), you need positive chain drive with zero slip, or <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet handling</a> is the primary application.</p>
<p><strong>Choose <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> instead when:</strong> no powered transport is needed, a slight decline is available, and budget is the top constraint.</p>
<h2 id="mfg-heading">MDR Roller Manufacturers</h2>
<p>The MDR supply chain has two tiers: component manufacturers (who make the motorized rollers) and system integrators (who build complete conveyor systems).</p>
<h3>MDR Roller Manufacturers</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Interroll</strong>, RollerDrive EC5000 series. Swiss-based global leader. Supplies motorized rollers to most major conveyor integrators.</li>
<li><strong>Itoh Denki</strong>, PM600/PM500 series. Japanese manufacturer with strong presence in Asia and North America.</li>
<li><strong>Rulmeca</strong>, RDR (RollerDrive) series. Italian manufacturer with global distribution.</li>
</ul>
<h3>System Integrators Using MDR</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hytrol</strong>, EZLogic MDR platform with proprietary zone control logic.</li>
<li><strong>Dematic</strong>, Integrated MDR conveyor systems for distribution and fulfillment.</li>
<li><strong>Intelligrated (Honeywell)</strong>, MDR accumulation and transport in IntelliSort systems.</li>
<li><strong>TGW</strong>, KingDrive MDR platform for e-commerce and retail distribution.</li>
<li><strong>Bastian Solutions (Toyota)</strong>, MDR conveyor design and integration for fulfillment centers.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What does MDR stand for?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>MDR stands for Motor Driven Roller. A 24VDC brushless DC motor sits inside the roller tube, creating an independent drive zone. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor hub</a> for all roller types.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What are the advantages over belt-driven roller?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Built-in ZPA without pneumatics, 50%+ energy savings, quieter (55-65 dB), less maintenance, easier expansion. Higher upfront cost but lower total cost of ownership. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller comparison</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What weight can MDR handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Standard MDR: 50-250 lbs per zone. For heavier loads, use <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">belt-driven live roller</a> (up to 500 lbs) or <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> (up to 10,000 lbs).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How does MDR zone control work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Each zone has one motorized roller, 2-4 slave rollers, a photo-eye sensor, and a zone controller card. The controller reads the sensor and communicates with adjacent zones. When downstream is occupied, the upstream zone holds its product with a gap, creating <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Who manufactures MDR rollers?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Interroll (RollerDrive EC5000), Itoh Denki (PM series), and Rulmeca (RDR series) manufacture the rollers. Hytrol, Dematic, Intelligrated, TGW, and Bastian Solutions integrate them into complete systems.</p>

</div>
